# The room editor (olc.c's redit, do_redit, the reset commands; olc_act.c's redit_* and change_exit) and the string
# editor (string.c), with Planner (security 9) in Midgaard, and Delver (security 0).
# A line of just "@" ends the string editor, but the scenario reads a line starting with @ as its own, so those
# lines are "x", a backspace and "@": the game's input takes the backspace away, as it does from a telnet client.
# - edit room in the temple; show; commands; rlist, mlist, olist (by name and by type), and their refusals;
#   mshow and oshow's refusals (showing one needs the mob and object editors)
# - name; desc in the string editor: every dot command, a ~ (smash_tilde makes it a -, so it's a line, not the
#   end), the end; format word-wraps it
# - heal, mana, clan, owner, room flags and sector, with their refusals; extra descriptions: add, edit, format,
#   delete, and keywords not found
# - exits: dig (a new room, linked both ways), name, key (refused, then a key), flags (copied to the other side),
#   desc, link and room (one way) and delete (both sides), with each refusal; walking through one changes the
#   room edited
# - .f on sentences, parentheses, quotes, long lines and a word too long for a line ("No spaces")
# - mreset and oreset (into the room, a container, a mob's hand and inventory); "already equipped" for anything
#   in the inventory, since WEAR_NONE matches it (so a shopkeeper, who carries his stock, can only be given
#   things to wear or hold); a shopkeeper's goods; each refusal
# - resets: list, add (mob, object in the room, inside, worn, given, random exits) and delete, and each refusal;
#   an empty position means "inside" object 1, which isn't a container; a reset naming no object isn't shown
#   (its line is made and not sent); a pet shop's mobs show as P, a shopkeeper's goods as S; a new room has no
#   resets
# - redit reset, create and a vnum; asave changed saves Midgaard
# - Delver can't edit, reset or list resets here
